# 8. Original-plan corrections that remain binding

## 8.1 BACKUP-04 does not own a format bump

`BUG-BACKUP-04` owns capture correctness: selected-category capture failure must not silently become an empty successful payload. Do not introduce a new backup format solely to close that defect.

## 8.2 Backup format evolution belongs to the first genuine payload extension

Preferred historical ordering:

- BACKUP-04: capture semantics only;
- BACKUP-05: first actual payload extension if a capability-bearing format is needed;
- BACKUP-07: extend that capability model for playlists/groups.

Do not bump format versions once per defect.

## 8.3 Never invent legacy backup semantics

Before interpreting formats 1–3 or unversioned payloads, inspect repository history/tests/artifacts. Unknown historical input must fail safely rather than be guessed into a schema.

## 8.4 Unmappable replacement markers must fail safely before BACKUP-03 exists

A stale backup-local History ID must never remain executable. Before a fully validated pre-mutation Reset plan exists, preserve affected rows only in a safe non-running diagnosable state rather than throwing a late restore-wide exception after partial mutation.

## 8.5 BACKUP-03 design must not preselect Room schema migration

Required semantics are atomic/fail-safe restore, not a specific persistence mechanism. Add a Room migration only if the smallest correct durable coordinator genuinely requires schema.

## 8.6 Playlist identity must not be invented

Imported playlists receive fresh destination IDs with explicit old→new maps. Do not merge by backup numeric ID, name, or `(name, description)` unless current repository semantics have since added a stable identity contract.

PlaylistGroup may use its actual unique-name DB constraint if still current.

## 8.7 Monthly cleanup means calendar month

A monthly cadence advances one calendar month, not a fixed 30-day interval.

